4. The registered log-linearity test on the truncation deficit

   REGISTRATION-CONVERGED.md, second verdict: d(n) is the within-trajectory
   ratio expected_max(peak_weights, 16) / expected_max(legacy_weight, 16)
   per instance, its error the standard error over instances; the test is
   weighted least squares of ln d(n) against n. A deficit whose logarithm
   is linear in n cancels from the steepening statistic; one whose logarithm
   is not does not, and the cancellation defence falls with it.

   n  inst      d(n)       ln d       +/-
   8    18    1.0005    0.00049   0.00019
  10    18    1.0140    0.01394   0.00173
  12    18    1.0427    0.04178   0.00364
  14    18    1.0705    0.06811   0.00732
  16    18    1.1051    0.09997   0.01359

   WLS of ln d against n: chi2 = 16.04 on 3 degrees of freedom, p = 0.0011
   VERDICT: log-linearity rejected at p < 0.05; per the registration, the
   cancellation sentence leaves Sec. VI A and Appendix B and the argument
   is not made in any other form.

5. The gradient paradox: a small gradient does not certify convergence

   Traces: analysis/optimizer_pacing.npz, n = 10 instance 0, 12 Adam
   restarts under the frozen protocol; the stepsize is halved at step 300.

                                    quantity     median                 IQR
   collapse of |grad|/delta, step 300 -> end        49x           32x - 64x
             delta gained over the same span      0.41%       0.16% - 0.87%

   Still available at n = 10 on the campaign's own seeds, from
   results/step_budget (3 instances, 400 -> 1600 steps): 1.35% on the
   restart mean and 2.27% on the instance best, up to 6.79% on one
   instance. The deficit grows with n; analysis/step_budget_control.py
   prints it per size.

   So the relative gradient falls by more than an order of magnitude while
   delta moves a few tenths of a percent, at a point where a longer budget
   still buys percent-level gains. The gradient tracks the stepsize, not the
   distance left to go: after a decay the iterate simply stops moving. This
   is why the converged protocol stops on the gain per doubling and not on
   a gradient norm, and why the 1600-step control stalled -- its stepsize
   had decayed to nothing with no floor under it.

   What these traces cannot show: they stop at 400 steps, so the
   'gain still to come' plotted against the step is measured against the
   400-step value and is a LOWER BOUND on the true remaining gain. Traces
   run under the converged protocol, with the learning-rate floor, would be
   needed to draw the whole gap; they are not in this repository.

